WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Here’s a secret about AI agents.

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.610
The model never touches a tool.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.030
It writes a line of text, and your code

4
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.330
decides what that text makes happen.

5
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.700
Today, you write the code that reads it.

6
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.660
Today: reading a tool call, in Python.

7
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.550
When an agent’s model takes a turn,

8
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.890
it writes one of three things.

9
00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.630
A plan: one short sentence

10
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.970
about what it will do.

11
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.580
A tool call: a tool’s

12
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.160
name, then its details.

13
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.590
Or an answer for the person.

14
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.150
Here’s a call that Darsi

15
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.270
Tiny really wrote.

16
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.300
First comes the tool’s name, weather.

17
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.680
Straight after it, the details

18
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.180
sit in curly brackets.

19
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.400
That part is JSON, a way to

20
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.620
write data as text.

21
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.130
JSON writes data as names and

22
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.150
values inside curly brackets.

23
00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.150
Python reads it with json dot loads, which

24
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.570
turns the text into a dictionary.

25
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.420
Then args, place, gives you Kafi.

26
00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.970
So how do you split a call?

27
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.600
Find the first curly bracket.

28
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.880
Everything before it is the tool’s name.

29
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.230
Everything from it onwards is the JSON.

30
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.090
But not every text is a call.

31
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.260
An answer like Tomorrow in Kafi,

32
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.080
sunny, has no curly bracket at all.

33
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.610
Then find gives minus one, and

34
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.310
your function should return None:

35
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.970
there’s no tool to run.

36
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:35.740
Quick check!

37
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.750
You run json dot loads on

38
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.130
the details of a call.

39
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.930
What do you get back?

40
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:44.790
A dictionary.

41
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.680
json dot loads only turns text into

42
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.040
data: the keys are the details’

43
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.020
names, like place and day.

44
00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.470
It doesn’t run anything, and it

45
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.410
doesn’t check anything.

46
00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.820
Is Kafi a real village?

47
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.320
That’s for your code to check next.

48
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.000
Models make mistakes, so some calls

49
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.260
are broken: a missing bracket, a

50
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.260
quote in the wrong place.

51
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.320
Then json dot loads raises a

52
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.500
ValueError, and your agent crashes.

53
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.990
Unless you catch it, with try and except.

54
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.750
In the lab, Darsi Tiny runs

55
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.350
for real, on your phone.

56
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.350
Press One step, and watch it

57
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.570
write the plan, then the call.

58
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.990
Open What Darsi Tiny sees: the

59
00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.470
call is plain text, exactly what

60
00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.410
your function will split.

61
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:36.440
Your turn.

62
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.280
The model wrote: Tomorrow in

63
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.220
Kafi, sunny, high thirty-five.

64
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.300
What should your function return?

65
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.500
None: there’s no curly

66
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.400
bracket, so it’s an answer.

67
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.230
Remember: the model writes a

68
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.870
plan, a call or an answer.

69
00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.810
Split a call at its first curly bracket,

70
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.770
and read the details with json dot loads.

71
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.630
Catch broken calls instead of crashing.

72
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.700
Try it yourself: on paper, write five

73
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.880
lines an agent might produce: some

74
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.480
calls, some answers, one broken.

75
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.070
Swap with a friend.

76
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.080
They mark each line as a call, an answer

77
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.400
or broken, and name the tool in every

78
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:21.720
call.
